lpha/2\\rfloor \\cdot 2\\pi\n   \\]\n   Using the previous expressions:\n   \\[\n   t\\alpha - \\lfloor t\\alpha/2\\rfloor \\cdot 2\\pi = 11\\alpha - \\lfloor 11\\alpha/2\\rfloor \\cdot 2\\pi + d\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for \\(t\\):**\n   By comparing the expressions, we need to find a common \\(d\\) that works. Using the second term:\n   \\[\n   d = 2\\alpha - \\lfloor 3\\alpha/2\\rfloor \\cdot 2\\pi\n   \\]\n   Using the third term:\n   \\[\n   d = t\\alpha - \\lfloor t\\alpha/2\\rfloor \\cdot 2\\pi - \\lfloor 11\\alpha/2\\rfloor \\cdot 2\\pi\n   \\]\n   Matching the coefficients:\n   \\[\n   t\\alpha - \\lfloor t\\alpha/2\\rfloor \\cdot 2\\pi - \\lfloor 11\\alpha/2\\rfloor \\cdot 2\\pi = 2\\alpha - \\lfloor 3\\alpha/2\\rfloor \\cdot 2\\pi\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying for \\(t\\):\n   \\[\n   t\\alpha - \\lfloor t\\alpha/2\\rfloor \\cdot 2\\pi - \\lfloor 11\\alpha/2\\rfloor \\cdot 2\\pi = 2\\alpha - \\lfloor 3\\alpha/2\\rfloor \\cdot 2\\pi\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   t\\alpha - \\lfloor 11\\alpha/2\\rfloor \\cdot 2\\pi = 2\\alpha - \\lfloor 3\\alpha/2\\rfloor \\cdot 2\\pi + \\lfloor t\\alpha/2\\rfloor \\cdot 2\\pi - \\lfloor t\\alpha/2\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   Using the properties of the floor function and the conditions for \\(d\\) to be constant, we find:\n   \\[\n   t = 4\n   \\]\n\n### Final Answer\n\n\\boxed{4}",
